Ladybird builds a fresh fragment for every VTTCue.getCueAsHTML() call.
Blink uses VTTCue::CopyVTTNodeToDOMTree and WebKit uses
copyWebVTTNodeToDOMTree to populate a fresh fragment from a parsed cue.
Gecko uses TextTrackCue::GetCueAsHTML and its WebVTT parser wrapper.

Chrome 158.0.8127.37, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 preserved voice,
language, classes, ruby, and timestamps. Updating the cue text produced
a fresh fragment and left the earlier one intact. All three used the
cue’s own document in cross-window calls, even when the method came from
the other window. Ladybird follows them here. The specification names
the entry settings object’s responsible document.

Ladybird uses the HTML tokenizer for character references in cue text
and annotations. Blink calls ConsumeHTMLEntity from its VTT tokenizer.
WebKit calls consumeHTMLEntity from WebVTTTokenizer. Gecko decodes cue
text in the unescapeEntities helper inside parseContent.

Chrome 158.0.8127.37 and WebKit 26.5 both decoded the legacy reference
in &notit; in text and preserved the literal spelling in a voice
annotation. Firefox 151.0 also distinguished text from annotations,
but preserved numeric references in cue text and left all references
in the tested annotation untouched.

WebVTT section 6.4 requires text-context decoding even in annotations.
Ladybird uses attribute-context decoding there to preserve the literal
spelling of &notit; that all three browsers expose in a voice title.
